WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.640
On February 26th, mortgage rates did

00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:06.480
something that they haven't done since

00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:08.600
2022. They fell below 6% for the first

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:10.520
time in 3 and 1/2 years, and buyers were

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:12.560
finally coming off the sidelines.

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:14.320
Affordability was improving, inventory

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:16.160
was rising, and the housing market

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:17.920
finally felt like it was getting a

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:19.960
little bit of a pulse. 2 days later,

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:21.760
everything changed. Military strikes

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:23.880
were launched on Iran, and the housing

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:25.600
market, it got hit harder than anybody

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:27.240
expected, and not just because of

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:28.640
mortgage rates. But people, we get into

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:30.760
it at any point in the video, if you get

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:32.880
any value, do me a huge favor. Go ahead

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:34.400
and smash that like button. It helps me

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:35.800
out a ton, and it gets this video in

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:36.720
front of more people who need to see it.

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:38.200
All right, so let me give you the

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:40.600
timeline so you understand exactly what

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:42.320
happened. On February 26th, rates hit

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:44.800
5.98%,

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:46.720
an almost 4-year low, and it was a

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:48.560
psychological breakthrough. Buyers who

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:50.400
had been waiting for years felt like

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:52.800
they could finally make that move. And

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:54.960
on February 28th, the US and Israel

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:57.080
launched strikes on Iran. March 5th,

00:00:54.960 --> 00:01:00.040
just 1 week later, rates had already

00:00:57.080 --> 00:01:02.720
went back above 6%, and by March 12th,

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:04.519
rates climbed to 6.23%,

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:06.840
which is one of the sharpest single-week

00:01:04.519 --> 00:01:08.840
jumps that we've seen in months. By last

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:10.520
week, the average 30-year fixed hit

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:12.480
6.43%.

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:15.760
Mortgage applications collapsed to

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:18.680
10.5%, and refinance demand went down

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:21.920
14.6%. And as of today, the average

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:23.680
30-year fixed rate is sitting at 6.44%

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:25.600
according to Zillow. And according to

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:27.720
the Mortgage Bankers Association,

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:29.480
mortgage applications have now dropped

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:30.800
for the second consecutive week, and

00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:32.320
that shows buyers aren't just

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:34.560
hesitating, they're pulling out and

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:36.640
panicking. In less than 4 weeks, the

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:38.240
best housing market setup in years got

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:40.840
torched. Here's what most people don't

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:43.080
understand. The Iran war doesn't affect

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:45.320
housing through some vague uncertainty.

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:47.440
It is a very specific chain reaction,

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:49.440
and you need to know how it works. Step

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:51.600
one is that the war disrupts oil

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:53.920
markets. Iran controls the Strait of

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:56.000
Hormuz, the only maritime route to the

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:58.400
open ocean for the Persian Gulf. 20

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:00.160
million barrels of oil per day flow

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:02.080
through that single choke point, and

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:04.280
there is no alternative route. And when

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:06.480
that gets disrupted, the entire global

00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:08.720
energy market feels it immediately. And

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:11.880
it did. And after Israel struck Iran's

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:13.840
South Pars gas field, Iran retaliated by

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:16.600
targeting energy facilities across

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:19.120
Qatar, Saudi Arabia, Kuwait, and the

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:22.600
UAE. The response from oil markets was

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:25.160
instant. Crude oil surged above $119 a

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:27.560
barrel, which is a $10 jump in a single

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:29.000
morning, close to a 3 and 1/2 year high.

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:30.840
Now think about what that means. When

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:32.880
oil spikes like that, it doesn't just

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:35.360
hit you at the gas pump, it hits the

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:37.160
cost of manufacturing, transportation,

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:39.280
food production, literally everything.

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:41.040
The entire economy gets more expensive

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:43.520
overnight. All right, step two. When

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:45.920
inflation fears spike, bond investors

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:48.360
panic. They demand higher yields on the

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:50.360
US Treasury bonds to compensate for that

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:53.200
inflation risk. And the 10-year Treasury

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:55.520
yield, which had dropped to 3.96%

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:57.680
right before the war, jumped to 4.42

00:02:55.520 --> 00:02:59.280
within 4 weeks. Step three, mortgage

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:00.800
rates. Now here's something that most

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:02.600
people don't realize. The Federal

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:04.640
Reserve doesn't actually set your

00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:06.240
mortgage rate. Your lender does. But the

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:08.240
number that your lender lands on is

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:09.960
heavily influenced by the 10-year

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:11.920
Treasury yield, basically what the

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:13.720
government pays to borrow money over the

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:15.480
course of a decade. And when that yield

00:03:13.720 --> 00:03:16.960
goes up, mortgage rates tend to go up

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:18.840
with it. And in March, that yield

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:20.760
jumped. But what made it even worse is

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:23.080
the fact that there's a second layer

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:25.160
called a risk premium. Think of this as

00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:27.080
an extra cushion that lenders tack on

00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:29.120
top of the Treasury yield to protect

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:31.120
themselves when markets get volatile.

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:33.320
When uncertainty spikes like during a

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:35.520
war, that premium goes up, too. So

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:37.480
buyers got hit twice. The Treasury yield

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:39.120
went up, and the risk premium went up on

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:40.800
top of that, which is a double hit

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:42.400
landing at the same time. And Ken

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:44.440
Johnson, chair of real estate at the

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:46.200
University of Mississippi, said that

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:48.520
over the past 10 business days, that

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:51.040
dual surge in benchmark yields and risk

00:03:48.520 --> 00:03:52.760
premiums has eliminated nearly all room

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:54.680
for downward movement in mortgage

00:03:52.760 --> 00:03:57.160
pricing. And according to Johnson, that

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:59.280
combination could mean even higher rates

00:03:57.160 --> 00:04:01.000
in the short term. Step four, mortgage

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:02.520
applications collapse. For the week

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:04.760
ending in March 20th, the Mortgage

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:06.600
Bankers Association reported the total

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:09.040
mortgage loan application volume had

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:10.840
dropped 10 and 1/2% the second

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:13.520
consecutive weekly decline. On an

00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:15.600
unadjusted basis, applications fell 10%

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:17.320
as well. So the data is consistent no

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:19.120
matter how you slice it. And really, the

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:21.120
biggest driver is refinancing.

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:23.280
Homeowners who were hoping to lock in a

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:25.320
lower rate got priced out almost

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:28.280
overnight. Refinance applications

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:29.600
plunged 15% in a single week. So people

00:04:28.280 --> 00:04:32.040
who had been waiting for the right

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:35.000
moment to refinance watched that window

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:37.600
slam shut in real time. MBA Deputy Chief

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:38.960
Economist Joel Kan summed it up. Higher

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:41.240
mortgage rates, coupled with

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:43.520
affordability constraints and economic

00:04:41.240 --> 00:04:45.720
uncertainty, pushed some potential home

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:47.880
buyers to the sidelines. So that's the

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:49.920
chain. You've got oil to inflation to

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:52.000
Treasury yields to mortgage rates, and

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:53.760
then buyers, they disappear. And the

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:55.560
irony is the part that should make you

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:57.920
angry if you're a buyer. This wasn't

00:04:55.560 --> 00:04:59.640
supposed to happen in 2026. This year is

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:02.480
supposed to be the comeback year for

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:04.480
housing. Zillow had projected 4.26

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:06.440
million existing home sales this year,

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:09.360
which is a 4.3%

00:05:06.440 --> 00:05:11.600
increase over 2025, driven by improving

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:13.200
affordability, bringing more buyers back

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:15.480
into the market, and inventory was

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:18.840
rising. Price growth had slowed to just

00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:21.480
1.3% nationally in 2025, which is the

00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:23.120
weakest pace in 14 years. And rates were

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:25.080
falling towards levels that would open

00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:26.520
the market back up. Every indicator was

00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:28.760
pointing in the right direction. For the

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:30.840
first time in years, buyers had momentum

00:05:28.760 --> 00:05:32.880
on their side. It was set up perfectly.

00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:34.720
And then a war started, and that window

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:36.680
slammed shut. A senior economist at

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:38.840
realtor.com put it this way, "The market

00:05:36.680 --> 00:05:40.840
is caught between long-term improvements

00:05:38.840 --> 00:05:42.800
and sudden short-term instability." And

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:44.440
that's really a diplomatic way of saying

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:45.880
that buyers got the rug pulled out from

00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:47.680
underneath of them. So it's not just

00:05:45.880 --> 00:05:49.680
existing homes, new construction's

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:51.040
getting hit hard, too. KB Home, one of

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:54.040
the largest home builders in the

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:55.520
country, just reported their Q1 2026

00:05:54.040 --> 00:05:58.440
earnings, and the numbers are pretty

00:05:55.520 --> 00:06:00.400
ugly. Revenue was down 23% year over

00:05:58.440 --> 00:06:05.080
year. Diluted earnings per share

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:08.120
collapsed 65% from $1.49 down to $0.52,

00:06:05.080 --> 00:06:09.760
and homes delivered fell 14%. And to

00:06:08.120 --> 00:06:11.800
move inventory, they've been cutting

00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:15.240
prices. The average selling price

00:06:11.800 --> 00:06:17.600
dropped from 500,000 to 452,000.

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:19.800
But what really stood out is their CEO,

00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:21.720
Rob McGibney, didn't just blame

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:24.440
affordability or interest rates. He

00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:26.880
pointed directly at the war. His words

00:06:24.440 --> 00:06:28.960
exactly on the earnings call were, "The

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:30.560
conflict in the Middle East started

00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:32.360
right at the end of February, and we saw

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:34.440
pretty good sales results in the first

00:06:32.360 --> 00:06:36.240
week of March. But the last couple of

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:38.040
weeks have been a little bit softer than

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:39.880
we would like to see." He added that

00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:42.000
they just don't have a lot of visibility

00:06:39.880 --> 00:06:44.320
right now on how long the conflict will

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:46.600
last or how it will weigh on consumer

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:49.200
confidence. A major home builder on a

00:06:46.600 --> 00:06:50.760
public earnings call literally telling

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:53.280
Wall Street that a war in the Middle

00:06:50.760 --> 00:06:55.160
East is killing their sales in Arizona,

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:56.280
California, and Texas. So here's

00:06:55.160 --> 00:06:58.040
something that doesn't show up in

00:06:56.280 --> 00:07:00.320
mortgage rate numbers, but matters just

00:06:58.040 --> 00:07:02.120
as much. Consumer confidence is

00:07:00.320 --> 00:07:04.560
collapsing. And even before the war

00:07:02.120 --> 00:07:07.160
started, buyers were canceling contracts

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:08.880
at the highest rate since 2017. Roughly

00:07:07.160 --> 00:07:10.960
one in seven homes that went under

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:13.720
contract in February were canceled,

00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:15.960
which is nearly 14% of deals falling

00:07:13.720 --> 00:07:17.600
apart before the Iran situation even

00:07:15.960 --> 00:07:20.720
fully hit. And right now, there is

00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:22.760
630,000 more home sellers than buyers,

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:24.560
the biggest gap on record, according to

00:07:22.760 --> 00:07:26.200
Redfin. Buyers are scared, and they're

00:07:24.560 --> 00:07:28.360
not just scared because they read an

00:07:26.200 --> 00:07:30.040
economics report, they're scared because

00:07:28.360 --> 00:07:32.000
they're watching the news, they're

00:07:30.040 --> 00:07:33.920
seeing oil prices, and they're seeing

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:35.800
gas at the pump, they're worried about

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:37.880
their jobs, and they're thinking, "Is

00:07:35.800 --> 00:07:39.400
now really the right time to take on a

00:07:37.880 --> 00:07:41.400
half a million-dollar mortgage?" And

00:07:39.400 --> 00:07:43.240
that psychology matters, and it's

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:45.400
weighing on the market in ways that are

00:07:43.240 --> 00:07:46.960
harder to quantify, but they are very

00:07:45.400 --> 00:07:48.400
real. Now here's something that most

00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:50.800
people covering the story are not

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:52.880
talking about. War can actually lower

00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:54.720
mortgage rates. Now it sounds backwards,

00:07:52.880 --> 00:07:57.560
but I'll tell you why. Normally, when

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:59.680
global chaos erupts, investors flee to

00:07:57.560 --> 00:08:01.880
safety. And the safest asset in the

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:04.240
world is the US Treasury bond. When

00:08:01.880 --> 00:08:06.400
demand for Treasuries surges, yields

00:08:04.240 --> 00:08:08.640
drop, and lower yields mean lower

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:10.480
mortgage rates. And that has been what's

00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:12.760
happened in past conflicts. Money

00:08:10.480 --> 00:08:14.520
flowing into bonds pushed rates down.

00:08:12.760 --> 00:08:16.760
But this time, the opposite happened,

00:08:14.520 --> 00:08:18.760
and the reason is because of inflation.

00:08:16.760 --> 00:08:20.600
Redfin Chief Economist explained the

00:08:18.760 --> 00:08:22.920
tension directly. "Fear of higher

00:08:20.600 --> 00:08:25.520
inflation because of higher oil prices

00:08:22.920 --> 00:08:27.920
tends to push rates up, but fear about

00:08:25.520 --> 00:08:30.480
the global stability and economic growth

00:08:27.920 --> 00:08:32.520
tends to push rates down. The best

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:35.120
strategy," she said, "is to lock in your

00:08:32.520 --> 00:08:37.800
rate at the right moment in time. If you

00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:40.560
see rates reach a low point, then call

00:08:37.800 --> 00:08:42.680
your lender and secure that rate." Okay,

00:08:40.560 --> 00:08:44.320
so before we go full doom and gloom, let

00:08:42.680 --> 00:08:46.400
me give you a little bit of perspective.

00:08:44.320 --> 00:08:48.040
Even at 6.44%,

00:08:46.400 --> 00:08:49.840
buyers today are better off than they

00:08:48.040 --> 00:08:52.760
were a year ago. 1 [snorts] year ago,

00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:54.920
the 30-year fixed was 6.66. And

00:08:52.760 --> 00:08:58.360
nationally, home values growth slowed to

00:08:54.920 --> 00:09:00.480
just 1.3% in 2025, which is the weakest

00:08:58.360 --> 00:09:03.040
pace in 14 years. And we haven't seen

00:09:00.480 --> 00:09:05.000
price growth this low since 2011, which

00:09:03.040 --> 00:09:07.400
means that sellers no longer have the

00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:09.400
pricing power that they had in 2022.

00:09:07.400 --> 00:09:11.080
Buyers have more options, and in most

00:09:09.400 --> 00:09:13.040
markets right now, buyers still have

00:09:11.080 --> 00:09:15.000
some negotiating leverage that they have

00:09:13.040 --> 00:09:16.840
not had in many years. Now on the

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:18.880
diplomatic front, here's the honest

00:09:16.840 --> 00:09:21.160
truth. The situation is complicated, and

00:09:18.880 --> 00:09:24.200
it's moving fast. The US submitted a

00:09:21.160 --> 00:09:26.720
15-point peace proposal to Iran through

00:09:24.200 --> 00:09:29.160
Pakistan as an intermediary, and Iran's

00:09:26.720 --> 00:09:31.680
response was they reviewed it and

00:09:29.160 --> 00:09:33.680
rejected it. A senior Iranian official

00:09:31.680 --> 00:09:36.360
told Reuters the proposal lacked the

00:09:33.680 --> 00:09:38.960
minimum requirements for success, and

00:09:36.360 --> 00:09:41.280
said no plan for talks appears realistic

00:09:38.960 --> 00:09:43.920
at this stage. Meanwhile, oil prices

00:09:41.280 --> 00:09:46.720
swung back up to around $100 a barrel on

00:09:43.920 --> 00:09:48.600
Thursday as markets digested the mixed

00:09:46.720 --> 00:09:50.680
signals. And markets are watching every

00:09:48.600 --> 00:09:52.400
headline. Bond yields are moving with

00:09:50.680 --> 00:09:54.480
every update. Until there's a real

00:09:52.400 --> 00:09:56.400
resolution, not just talk of one,

00:09:54.480 --> 00:09:58.160
mortgage rates are most likely going to

00:09:56.400 --> 00:10:00.440
stay volatile. Here's what you need to

00:09:58.160 --> 00:10:03.600
be watching. First is the diplomatic

00:10:00.440 --> 00:10:05.640
situation. As of today, the US and Iran

00:10:03.600 --> 00:10:07.640
are exchanging proposals through

00:10:05.640 --> 00:10:09.760
intermediaries. Now, whether that leads

00:10:07.640 --> 00:10:12.000
anywhere is genuinely unknown. Trump

00:10:09.760 --> 00:10:14.080
says that talks are productive and Iran

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:16.440
says that no deal is realistic at this

00:10:14.080 --> 00:10:18.400
stage. So, both sides are still firing

00:10:16.440 --> 00:10:20.320
and until there's an actual resolution,

00:10:18.400 --> 00:10:22.600
not just talk of one, oil's going to

00:10:20.320 --> 00:10:24.600
stay volatile and volatile oil means

00:10:22.600 --> 00:10:26.520
volatile mortgage rates. Second, if

00:10:24.600 --> 00:10:29.080
energy-driven inflation continues to

00:10:26.520 --> 00:10:30.920
rise, the Fed delays rate cuts and

00:10:29.080 --> 00:10:32.960
delayed rate cuts mean elevated

00:10:30.920 --> 00:10:35.120
borrowing costs through the fall and

